Equitable Genomic Medicine: The Equity Feasibility Region as a Deployment-Admissibility Criterion

Abstract

Polygenic scores (PGS) and genomic risk prediction models are increasingly proposed for clinical deployment, yet their performance varies systematically across ancestral populations and social contexts. Recent evidence standards for multi-ancestry polygenic prediction (Oselu et al., Genome Biology 2026) advance a benchmarking paradigm spanning discrimination, calibration, equity gaps, and computational cost. This paper argues that benchmarking and admissibility are distinct epistemic operations. Benchmarking measures; admissibility constrains. We propose the Equity Feasibility Region \mathcal{V}_E as a formal decision-theoretic object that transforms equity from a post hoc evaluation statistic into a pre-specified condition governing progression from genomic inference to clinical and economic deployment. Crucially, we decompose the admissibility parameter into model properties \theta_M and deployment-system properties \theta_S, so that the same genomic model may be admissible in one health system and inadmissible in another. We extend the static formulation to a dynamic viability condition \theta_t \in \mathcal{V}_E(t), in which the feasibility region evolves as new evidence accumulates. We also specify a three-party governance mechanism for setting the tolerance vector \boldsymbol{\delta}, without which the distinction between benchmarking and admissibility collapses. The individual statistical components—spectral linear mixed models, ancestry-specific quality control, stratified false discovery rate control, local ancestry inference—are established prior art and are not claimed as novel. We correct several technical formulations, including the harmonic mean p-value permutation calibration, the scoping of DeLong variance bias, and the local ancestry attenuation formula. The contribution is architectural: the composition of these components into an auditable, pre-specified, and dynamically updated admissibility criterion that governs progression from genomic discovery to clinical and economic deployment.

