1. Introduction

1.1 The Portability Problem

Polygenic scores are prospective tools for health screening, prevention, and clinical trials, but most are trained in European genome-wide association studies and lose accuracy and calibration in non-European and admixed populations (Martin et al., Nature Genetics 2019). The All of Us research program, using 245,388 whole-genome sequences, has demonstrated that increased diversity can significantly improve PGS performance in underrepresented individuals across multiple phenotypes (Bick et al., Nature 2024). Performance is not adequately described by discrete ancestry categories: PGS accuracy declines continuously with genetic distance from the training population (Ding et al., Nature 2023), with a Pearson correlation of approximately -0.95 across 84 traits. More recent work shows that individual-level PGS accuracy is only weakly predicted by genetic distance and is comparably well explained by socioeconomic measures, reinforcing that ancestry is a genetically inferred construct while equity is a normative construct encompassing access, utility, and social determinants of health.

1.2 The Benchmarking Paradigm and Its Limits

The 2026 evidence standards for multi-ancestry polygenic prediction propose a scorecard spanning discrimination, calibration, equity gaps, and compute cost, plus stress tests in diverse cohorts (Oselu et al., Genome Biology 2026). This framework represents a significant advance over ad hoc performance reporting. However, benchmarking and admissibility are not equivalent. A benchmark answers the question: How does this model perform under specified conditions? An admissibility criterion answers a different question: Under what conditions may this model be permitted to proceed to the next stage of deployment?

The distinction is not merely semantic. A model may perform well on every benchmark dimension and still be inadmissible if the benchmark tolerances were not pre-specified, if the evaluation cohort is not representative of the deployment population, or if the evidence base is insufficient to bound the model's behavior under distributional shift. Conversely, a model may fail a particular benchmark threshold yet remain admissible under a more permissive pre-specified criterion that reflects the clinical context. The benchmarking paradigm, as currently formulated, does not resolve which errors are tolerable and which are disqualifying; it provides the measurements, not the decision rule.

1.3 The Proposal: Equity as a Formal Constraint

This paper advances the following architectural thesis:

\boxed{
\text{Benchmarking measures disparities. Admissibility constrains deployment.}
}

The central formal object is the Equity Feasibility Region \mathcal{V}_E, a pre-specified subset of model-parameter space within which a genomic model is deemed admissible for progression from discovery to clinical and economic evaluation. The condition

\theta \in \mathcal{V}_E

is not an evaluation statistic. It is a constraint on the model's deployment trajectory. This transforms equity from an after-the-fact descriptive property into a constitutive condition on what counts as a valid genomic model for a given clinical purpose.

We decompose the admissibility parameter as

\theta = (\theta_M, \theta_S),

where \theta_M parameterizes the genomic model (training population, target population, trait, feature set, calibration procedure) and \theta_S parameterizes the deployment system (health system, insurance coverage, provider incentives, patient uptake). This decomposition is necessary because access and utility disparities depend jointly on both. A model with small discrimination disparity deployed in a system with no reimbursement for the target ancestry will exhibit large access disparity despite being admissibly fair in the discrimination sense. The Equity Feasibility Region is therefore defined over the pair:

\mathcal{V}_E(\boldsymbol{\delta}) = \left\{ (\theta_M, \theta_S) \in \Theta_M \times \Theta_S : \bigwedge_{r=1}^{R} \Delta_r(\theta_M, \theta_S) \leq \delta_r \right\},

where the \Delta_r are operational equity dimensions—discrimination, calibration, false discovery rate, access, and utility—and the \delta_r are pre-specified tolerances.

1.4 Dynamic Viability

The static condition \theta \in \mathcal{V}_E answers whether a model-context pair passes today's admissibility criterion. A deployed system operates over time; populations change, evidence accumulates, and calibration drifts. The static formulation is therefore incomplete for sustained clinical use. We extend the framework to a dynamic viability condition. Let D_t denote the evidence base at time t, a filtration with D_t \subseteq D_{t+1}. Define

\mathcal{V}_E(t) = \left\{ (\theta_M, \theta_S) : \Delta_r(\theta_M, \theta_S, D_t) \leq \delta_r, \; r = 1,\ldots,R \right\}.

A deployed pair (\theta_M, \theta_S)_t is dynamically admissible at time t if

\boxed{(\theta_M, \theta_S)_t \in \mathcal{V}_E(t).}

When new evidence D_{\text{new}} arrives,

D_{t+1} = D_t \cup D_{\text{new}}, \qquad \mathcal{V}_E(t+1) = \mathcal{F}(\mathcal{V}_E(t), D_{\text{new}}),

where \mathcal{F} is the update operator. Dynamic viability is a trajectory constraint: a model admissible at t = 0 may become inadmissible at t = T if the feasibility region contracts faster than the model can adapt. This poses a control-theoretic question—under what evidence dynamics does a deployed system remain inside its admissibility region?—that is not addressed by existing dynamic PRS methods, which focus on prediction accuracy rather than admissibility.

1.5 The Governance of \boldsymbol{\delta}

The framework's entire normative force rests on \boldsymbol{\delta} being pre-specified, not reverse-engineered from results. But the framework is silent on who sets \boldsymbol{\delta}, by what process, and with what legitimacy. Without addressing this, a sponsor can shop for lenient \boldsymbol{\delta} before the trial begins and label the result "pre-specified." We propose a three-party deliberation structure: a regulatory floor \boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{reg}} that no admissibility criterion may exceed, a clinical-society specification \boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{clin}} \geq \boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{reg}} within the regulatory floor, and an affected-community ratification \boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{comm}} \geq \boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{clin}} with authority to tighten but never to loosen below the regulatory floor. The admissible set is \mathcal{V}_E(\boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{comm}}). Formalizing the incentive properties of this structure is an open problem, but the structure itself is necessary to make the distinction between benchmarking and admissibility substantive.

1.6 Contributions and Scope

This paper makes one architectural contribution and several corrective ones.

Architectural. We introduce the Equity Feasibility Region \mathcal{V}_E as a formal decision-theoretic object governing deployment admissibility. The central thesis is that benchmarking measures disparities while admissibility constrains deployment, and these are not equivalent operations. The framework is developed in three layers: (i) the static admissibility condition \theta \in \mathcal{V}_E; (ii) the dynamic viability condition \theta_t \in \mathcal{V}_E(t); and (iii) the governance mechanism for setting \boldsymbol{\delta}.

Corrective. We fix technical errors in prior formulations, including the harmonic mean p-value permutation calibration (invalid due to permutation invariance), the scoping of DeLong variance bias (regime-specific, not universal), the local ancestry attenuation formula (analytic expression replacing a numerical range), and the column-dimension loss in corrected minor allele frequency calculation.

Explicitly prior art. Spectral linear mixed models (Lippert et al., Nature Methods 2011), Tractor (Atkinson et al., Nature Genetics 2021), Logica (2025), DeLong variance (DeLong et al., Biometrics 1988), harmonic mean p-values (Wilson, PNAS 2019), independent hypothesis weighting (Ignatiadis et al., Nature Methods 2016), and equity-weighted QALYs (Bleichrodt et al., Journal of Health Economics 2004) are established prior art and are not claimed as novel.

1.7 Organization

Section 2 develops the admissibility parameter and the decomposition \theta = (\theta_M, \theta_S). Section 3 formalizes the Equity Feasibility Region and distinguishes it from benchmarking. Section 4 addresses the governance of \boldsymbol{\delta}. Section 5 provides the statistical inference procedures for the region's constraints. Section 6 develops the dynamic viability formulation. Section 7 positions the economic layer as a downstream application. Section 8 concludes with open problems in mechanism design, trajectory viability, and transportability of deployment-system parameters.

