The framework does not resolve the fundamental tension between statistical optimality and equitable access, but it makes that tension explicit and subject to formal analysis. In doing so, it provides a foundation for genomic medicine that treats equity not as an afterthought but as a constitutive condition on what counts as a valid genomic model.


Chapter 2: The Admissibility Parameter

2.1 Decomposition of θ

Prior formulations treat the admissibility parameter as a model property. This is incomplete. We decompose

\theta = (\theta_M, \theta_S),

where \theta_M parameterizes the genomic model and \theta_S parameterizes the deployment system.

Definition 2.1 (Model Parameter). The model parameter is

\theta_M = (\mathcal{P}_{\text{train}}, \mathcal{P}_{\text{target}}, \mathcal{T}, \mathcal{F}, \mathcal{C}),

where \mathcal{P}_{\text{train}} is the training population, \mathcal{P}_{\text{target}} is the target population, \mathcal{T} is the trait, \mathcal{F} is the feature set, and \mathcal{C} is the calibration procedure.

Definition 2.2 (Deployment-System Parameter). The deployment-system parameter is

\theta_S = (\mathcal{R}, \mathcal{T}_{\text{prov}}, \mathcal{O}, \mathcal{I}),

where \mathcal{R} is the reimbursement policy, \mathcal{T}_{\text{prov}} is the provider training and incentive structure, \mathcal{O} is the patient outreach program, and \mathcal{I} is the insurance coverage regime.

Remark 2.1. The decomposition is not merely formal. It implies that admissibility is a property of the pair (\theta_M, \theta_S), not of the model alone. Two health systems deploying the same PRS may reach different verdicts. This is empirically grounded in recent work showing that individual-level PGS accuracy is comparably well explained by socioeconomic measures as by genetic distance.

2.2 Equity Dimensions

Let \Delta_r : \Theta_M \times \Theta_S \to \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0} for r = 1, \dots, R. We specify R = 5 operational dimensions.

Definition 2.3 (Equity Dimensions).

Dimension Metric Constraint
\Delta_1 discrimination \max_{A,B} \|\text{AUC}_A - \text{AUC}_B\| \leq \delta_1
\Delta_2 calibration \max_{A,B} \|\text{CalErr}_A - \text{CalErr}_B\| \leq \delta_2
\Delta_3 FDR \max_{A,B} \|\text{FDR}_A - \text{FDR}_B\| \leq \delta_3
\Delta_4 access \max_{A,B} \|\text{Coverage}_A(\theta_S) - \text{Coverage}_B(\theta_S)\| \leq \delta_4
\Delta_5 utility \max_{A,B} \|\text{NetBenefit}_A(\theta_S) - \text{NetBenefit}_B(\theta_S)\| \leq \delta_5

The dependence of \Delta_4 and \Delta_5 on \theta_S is explicit: coverage and net clinical benefit are functions of reimbursement, provider behavior, and patient uptake, not of the model alone.

Remark 2.2 (Ancestry Is Not Equity). Ancestry performance gaps and equity gaps are not mathematically identical. Ancestry is a genetically inferred construct; equity is a normative construct encompassing access, utility, and social determinants of health. The feasibility region is therefore defined over the full set of dimensions, with ancestry being one source of heterogeneity, not the definition of equity.

