Chapter 3: The Equity Feasibility Region

3.1 Definition

Definition 3.1 (Equity Feasibility Region). Given pre-specified tolerances \boldsymbol{\delta} = (\delta_1, \ldots, \delta_R) \in \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^R,

\mathcal{V}_E(\boldsymbol{\delta}) = \left\{ (\theta_M, \theta_S) \in \Theta_M \times \Theta_S : \bigwedge_{r=1}^{R} \Delta_r(\theta_M, \theta_S) \leq \delta_r \right\}.

A model-context pair is admissible for progression if and only if (\theta_M, \theta_S) \in \mathcal{V}_E(\boldsymbol{\delta}).

3.2 Benchmarking and Admissibility Are Not Equivalent

Let

\mathcal{B}(\theta, D) = (\Delta_1(\theta, D), \ldots, \Delta_R(\theta, D))

be the benchmarking operator and

\mathcal{A}(\theta, D, \boldsymbol{\delta}) = \mathbf{1}\{ \theta \in \mathcal{V}_E(\boldsymbol{\delta}) \}

the admissibility operator.

Remark 3.1 (Definitional unpacking, not a theorem). Two observations follow directly from the definitions.

(i) If \boldsymbol{\delta} is not pre-specified, then \mathcal{A} is undefined even though \mathcal{B} is defined. Benchmarking does not entail admissibility.

(ii) If \boldsymbol{\delta} is pre-specified but omits a benchmark B^*, a model can satisfy \mathcal{A} while failing B^*. Admissibility does not require passing all benchmarks.

Remark 3.2. This is a clarifying observation, not a theorem. Its content is wholly in the definitions of \mathcal{B} and \mathcal{A}. It is stated here to make the distinction explicit; it should not be presented as a formal result. The substantive claim is normative: the field has been treating benchmarking as if it were admissibility, and this conflation has consequences for deployment decisions.

3.3 Monotonicity

Remark 3.3. If \boldsymbol{\delta} \leq \boldsymbol{\delta}' componentwise, then \mathcal{V}_E(\boldsymbol{\delta}) \subseteq \mathcal{V}_E(\boldsymbol{\delta}'). This follows immediately from the definition.

3.4 Intersection Structure

Definition 3.2. Define \mathcal{V}_E^{(r)} = \{(\theta_M, \theta_S) : \Delta_r(\theta_M, \theta_S) \leq \delta_r\}. Then

\mathcal{V}_E = \bigcap_{r=1}^{R} \mathcal{V}_E^{(r)}.

Remark 3.4. Each \mathcal{V}_E^{(r)} is a half-space in the \Delta_r direction. \mathcal{V}_E is the intersection of R half-spaces, a convex polytope when the \Delta_r are convex functions of \theta.

