Chapter 4: Governance of δ

4.1 The Governance Gap

The framework's entire normative force rests on \boldsymbol{\delta} being pre-specified, not reverse-engineered from results. But the framework is silent on who sets \boldsymbol{\delta}, by what process, and with what legitimacy. Without addressing this, a sponsor can shop for lenient \boldsymbol{\delta} before the trial begins and label the result "pre-specified." This is the governance gap that determines whether \mathcal{V}_E does the work it is designed to do.

4.2 Three-Party Deliberation

We propose that \boldsymbol{\delta} be set by a three-party deliberation with the following structural properties.

Definition 4.1 (Three-Party δ-Setting).

1. Regulatory floor. A regulator (e.g., FDA, EMA, NICE) sets a minimum floor \boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{reg}} \in \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^R that no admissibility criterion may exceed.
2. Clinical-society specification. A clinical society (e.g., AACR, ASHG) specifies \boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{clin}} \geq \boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{reg}} within the regulatory floor, subject to public comment.
3. Affected-community ratification. A standing panel including patient advocates from historically under-served ancestries ratifies \boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{comm}} \geq \boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{clin}}, with authority to tighten but never to loosen below the regulatory floor.

The admissible set is

\mathcal{V}_E(\boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{comm}}) = \left\{ \theta : \bigwedge_{r=1}^R \Delta_r(\theta) \leq \delta_{\text{comm},r} \right\}.

4.3 Mechanism-Design Analysis

We model the three-party process as a sequential game with players: sponsor S, clinical society C, and community panel P. The sponsor chooses \theta_M to minimize cost subject to \theta \in \mathcal{V}_E(\boldsymbol{\delta}). The clinical society chooses \boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{clin}} to maximize clinical validity subject to \boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{clin}} \geq \boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{reg}}. The community panel chooses \boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{comm}} to minimize \Delta_{\text{access}} and \Delta_{\text{utility}} subject to \boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{comm}} \geq \boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{clin}}.

Proposition 4.1 (Exogeneity Condition). The three-party process produces \boldsymbol{\delta} credibly exogenous to the sponsor if and only if:

1. The sponsor cannot influence the selection of clinical-society members.
2. The sponsor cannot influence the selection of community-panel members.
3. The regulatory floor is binding: \delta_{\text{comm},r} \geq \delta_{\text{reg},r} for all r.
4. The community panel has authority to tighten \boldsymbol{\delta} without sponsor veto.

Justification. If conditions 1–2 fail, the sponsor can capture one or more parties and shift \boldsymbol{\delta} toward leniency. If condition 3 fails, the regulatory floor is non-binding and the sponsor can shop for a lenient clinical specification. If condition 4 fails, the community panel cannot correct for clinical-society capture. The conditions are jointly necessary and sufficient for exogeneity in the sense that no sponsor strategy can shift \boldsymbol{\delta} below the regulatory floor. \square

Remark 4.1. The proposition is a preliminary characterization. A full mechanism-design analysis would specify the sponsor's optimization problem, the clinical society's objective function, the community panel's objective function, and derive equilibrium \boldsymbol{\delta} under various informational assumptions. We flag this as an open problem in §8.

4.4 Why This Matters for the Central Thesis

The distinction between benchmarking and admissibility is only substantive if \boldsymbol{\delta} is set by a process that is independent of the model under evaluation. If a sponsor can participate in setting \boldsymbol{\delta}, the distinction collapses: the sponsor would choose \boldsymbol{\delta} to make \mathcal{V}_E contain their model. The three-party structure is designed to make \boldsymbol{\delta}-setting credibly exogenous to the sponsor.

This is a governance contribution, not a mathematical one. Formalizing the incentive properties of three-party \boldsymbol{\delta}-setting is an open problem (see §8.1).

