Chapter 5: Statistical Inference for the Feasibility Region

5.1 Within-Stratum FDR Control

For ancestry stratum a with m_a tests, the Benjamini–Hochberg procedure at level \alpha controls

\text{FDR}_a = \mathbb{E}\left[\frac{|\mathcal{R}_a \cap \mathcal{H}_{0,a}|}{|\mathcal{R}_a| \vee 1}\right] \leq \alpha,

provided the p-values satisfy positive regression dependence on subsets (PRDS). Within a single ancestry stratum, local LD structure typically induces this condition.

5.2 Cross-Ancestry Harmonic Mean P-Values

The harmonic mean p-value is

\text{HMP} = \frac{k}{\sum_{a=1}^{k} 1/p_a}.

Proposition 5.1 (HMP Invariance). The HMP is invariant to permutation of the p-values:

\text{HMP}(p_1, \ldots, p_k) = \text{HMP}(p_{\pi(1)}, \ldots, p_{\pi(k)})

for any permutation \pi of \{1,\dots,k\}.

Proof. The denominator is a sum over all a, which is invariant to reordering. \square

Corollary 5.1 (Permutation Calibration Invalid). Permuting the p-values does not generate a valid null distribution for the HMP, since the permuted statistic equals the observed statistic.

Corrective Remark 5.1. Prior formulations invoking permutation calibration should be replaced by calibration against the asymptotic LogGamma null distribution. The calibrated p-value is

p_{\text{HMP}} = 1 - F_{\text{LogGamma}}(\text{HMP}; \alpha, \beta),

with parameters estimated via maximum likelihood under the null. The harmonicmeanp R package version 3.0 provides asymptotically exact p-values based on this distribution.

5.3 DeLong Variance: A Careful Statement

The DeLong nonparametric estimator of AUC variance is asymptotically correct under iid sampling. Its documented failure modes are not a universal positive bias but specific to (i) finite-sample regimes with AUC near 0.5 or 1.0, (ii) extreme case/control ratios, and (iii) violation of the assumption that the models being compared are fixed and evaluated on data independent of training.

Remark 5.2. We do not claim that DeLong's estimator is universally positively biased. We claim that, in the regime relevant to equity studies—small n_{\text{cases}} in under-served ancestries, AUC values near 0.5 or 1.0, and models trained and evaluated on the same data—the DeLong variance estimate can be substantially biased and should be treated with caution.

Proposition 5.2 (Delta-method alternative). If the AUC is expressed as a differentiable function \theta = g(\boldsymbol{\pi}) of the confusion-matrix probabilities \boldsymbol{\pi}, and if \hat{\boldsymbol{\pi}} is asymptotically normal with covariance \Sigma_{\boldsymbol{\pi}}, then

\operatorname{Var}(\hat{\theta}) \approx \nabla g(\hat{\boldsymbol{\pi}})^{\mathsf{T}} \Sigma_{\boldsymbol{\pi}} \nabla g(\hat{\boldsymbol{\pi}}).

This is the standard delta-method result. Its empirical advantage over DeLong in equity regimes is an empirical question, not a theoretical guarantee.

5.4 Simultaneous Equivalence Testing for k > 2

For k ancestry groups, define \mathcal{P} = \{(a,b) : 1 \leq a < b \leq k\}. The global discrimination-equity claim is

\forall (a,b) \in \mathcal{P}: |\text{AUC}_a - \text{AUC}_b| \leq \delta_1,

with simultaneous coverage 1 - \alpha. Equivalence via TOST with Bonferroni correction at level \alpha/|\mathcal{P}| per pair controls the family-wise error rate at level \alpha.

5.5 Local Ancestry Error: Correcting the Quantitative Claim

Let I = \mathbf{1}\{a_{ij} = a\} be the true local ancestry indicator and \hat{I} the estimated indicator. Under the measurement-error model with sensitivity se and specificity sp,

E[\hat{I}] = p \cdot se + (1-p)(1-sp), \qquad \operatorname{Cov}(\hat{I}, I) = p(1-p)(se + sp - 1).

The attenuation factor is

\frac{\hat{\beta}}{\beta} \to \frac{p(1-p)(se+sp-1)}{E[\hat{I}](1 - E[\hat{I}])}.

Corrective Remark 5.3. Prior formulations assert a "10–30%" bias range. This quantitative claim should be replaced by the analytic expression above, with simulation-derived magnitudes for specific (se, sp, p) regimes. Additionally, per-site accuracy does not guarantee tract-structure accuracy: methods with high per-site accuracy can produce tract-count inflation by a factor of \eta \approx 78.8 and admixture-time overestimation by 61.2\times at low genetic divergence.

