Chapter 7: Economic Layer as Downstream Application

7.1 Equity-Adjusted ICER

The economic layer is an application of \mathcal{V}_E, not an independent theoretical contribution. Given (\theta_M, \theta_S) \in \mathcal{V}_E, the equity-adjusted ICER is

\text{ICER}_{\text{equity}}(\epsilon) = \frac{C - C_0}{\sum_A w_A(\epsilon) Q_A - Q_0},

with Atkinson weights

w_A(\epsilon) = \frac{(\bar{h}/h_A)^\epsilon}{\sum_{A'} (\bar{h}/h_{A'})^\epsilon}.

Because \epsilon is ethically consequential and not determined by the data, results should be reported as a curve \epsilon \mapsto \text{ICER}_{\text{equity}}(\epsilon) rather than a point estimate.

Remark 7.1. The equity-weighted ICER is established health-economics practice. It is included here because the framework requires a downstream valuation of admissible models, not because it is novel.

7.2 Incentive Compatibility as Future Work

The step-function pricing rule

R(q) = R_{\text{base}}\left(1 + \alpha \mathbf{1}\{q \geq q_1\} + \beta \mathbf{1}\{q \geq q_2\} + \gamma \mathbf{1}\{q \geq q_3\}\right)

with incremental conditions \alpha R_{\text{base}} \geq C(q_1) - C(0), etc., establishes that q^* = q_3 is globally optimal when C is nondecreasing. This is a straightforward consequence of monotone costs and stepwise rewards. The dynamic extension—where the developer's choice of q in period t affects reputation and future payoffs—remains an open problem and is positioned as future research rather than a core contribution.

