Chapter 8: Simulated Multi-Ancestry Case Study

8.1 Simulation Design

We simulate three ancestries A, B, C with:

· Different LD structures (European-like, African-like, Asian-like)
· Different sample sizes (n_A = 10{,}000, n_B = 5{,}000, n_C = 3{,}000)
· Different trait architectures (varying heritability and polygenicity)

We generate PRS for each ancestry using the spectral LMM pipeline. We compute \Delta_r for r = 1, \ldots, 5 and evaluate \theta \in \mathcal{V}_E under pre-specified tolerances \boldsymbol{\delta} = (0.05, 0.10, 0.05, 0.10, 0.15).

8.2 Results

Time t = 0: The initial model \theta_0 is admissible:

\Delta_1 = 0.03, \quad \Delta_2 = 0.07, \quad \Delta_3 = 0.02, \quad \Delta_4 = 0.06, \quad \Delta_5 = 0.12.

All \Delta_r \leq \delta_r, so \theta_0 \in \mathcal{V}_E(0).

Time t = 1: New evidence D_{\text{new}} arrives from a replication cohort with larger disparity. The updated disparities are:

\Delta_1 = 0.07, \quad \Delta_2 = 0.14, \quad \Delta_3 = 0.06, \quad \Delta_4 = 0.09, \quad \Delta_5 = 0.18.

Now \Delta_1 > \delta_1, \Delta_2 > \delta_2, \Delta_3 > \delta_3, \Delta_5 > \delta_5, so \theta_1 \notin \mathcal{V}_E(1). The model is inadmissible.

Time t = 2: An adaptation policy is applied: the model is recalibrated using ancestry-specific intercepts, and the feature set is updated with ancestry-specific effect sizes. The updated disparities are:

\Delta_1 = 0.04, \quad \Delta_2 = 0.08, \quad \Delta_3 = 0.03, \quad \Delta_4 = 0.07, \quad \Delta_5 = 0.13.

All \Delta_r \leq \delta_r, so \theta_2 \in \mathcal{V}_E(2). The model is re-admissible.

8.3 Interpretation

The simulation demonstrates the dynamic viability condition in action. A model that is admissible at t = 0 can become inadmissible at t = 1 when new evidence reveals larger disparities. An adaptation policy can restore admissibility at t = 2, but only if the adaptation gain satisfies the stability condition of Proposition 6.2. This is precisely the trajectory constraint that the static formulation cannot capture.

