Appendix B: Spectral LMM — Detailed Derivations and Complexity

B.1 Marginal Covariance and Rotation

The marginal model is

\mathbf{y} \sim \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{X}\boldsymbol{\beta}, \mathbf{V}),
\qquad
\mathbf{V} = \sigma_g^2 \mathbf{K} + \sigma_e^2 \mathbf{I}_n.

Let

\mathbf{K} = \mathbf{U} \mathbf{S} \mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{T}},
\qquad
\mathbf{S} = \operatorname{diag}(s_1,\dots,s_n).

Define rotated quantities:

\tilde{\mathbf{y}} = \mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{T}}\mathbf{y},
\qquad
\tilde{\mathbf{X}} = \mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{T}}\mathbf{X},
\qquad
\tilde{\mathbf{g}} = \mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{T}}\mathbf{g},
\qquad
\tilde{\boldsymbol{\epsilon}} = \mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{T}}\boldsymbol{\epsilon}.

Then

\tilde{\mathbf{g}} \sim \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0}, \sigma_g^2 \mathbf{S}),
\qquad
\tilde{\boldsymbol{\epsilon}} \sim \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0}, \sigma_e^2 \mathbf{I}_n).

Hence the rotated observations are independent:

\tilde{y}_i \sim \mathcal{N}\left(\tilde{\mathbf{X}}_{i,\cdot}\boldsymbol{\beta}, \sigma_g^2 s_i + \sigma_e^2\right).

B.2 Profile Likelihood

Let

\delta = \frac{\sigma_e^2}{\sigma_g^2}.

Then

\operatorname{Var}(\tilde{y}_i) = \sigma_g^2 (s_i + \delta).

The log-likelihood is

LL(\boldsymbol{\beta}, \sigma_g^2, \delta)
=
-\frac{1}{2}
\left[
n\log(2\pi)
+
n\log \sigma_g^2
+
\sum_{i=1}^{n}\log(s_i+\delta)
+
\frac{1}{\sigma_g^2}
\sum_{i=1}^{n}
\frac{(\tilde{y}_i - \tilde{\mathbf{X}}_{i,\cdot}\boldsymbol{\beta})^2}{s_i+\delta}
\right].

For fixed \delta, the MLE for \boldsymbol{\beta} is

\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}(\delta)
=
\left[
\sum_{i=1}^{n}
\frac{1}{s_i+\delta}
\tilde{\mathbf{X}}_{i,\cdot}^{\mathsf{T}}
\tilde{\mathbf{X}}_{i,\cdot}
\right]^{-1}
\left[
\sum_{i=1}^{n}
\frac{1}{s_i+\delta}
\tilde{\mathbf{X}}_{i,\cdot}^{\mathsf{T}}
\tilde{y}_i
\right].

The MLE for \sigma_g^2 is

\hat{\sigma}_g^2(\delta)
=
\frac{1}{n}
\sum_{i=1}^{n}
\frac{
(\tilde{y}_i - \tilde{\mathbf{X}}_{i,\cdot}\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}(\delta))^2
}{
s_i+\delta
}.

Substituting these into LL gives the profile log-likelihood as a function of \delta alone.

B.3 Complexity Correction

The standard LMM requires O(n^3 m) time because \mathbf{V}^{-1} must be recomputed or updated per SNP.

For the spectral LMM:

1. One-time eigendecomposition: O(n^3).
2. Rotation of the full genotype matrix \mathbf{G} \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times m}: O(n^2 m).
3. Per-SNP score test and variance update in the diagonalized space: O(n).
4. Summing over m SNPs: O(nm).

Therefore the total time complexity is

T(n,m)
=
O(n^3) + O(n^2 m) + O(nm)
=
O(n^3 + n^2 m).

The per-SNP cost is O(n) only after the genotype matrix has been rotated. The speedup over the standard O(n^3 m) remains of order n when m \gg n:

\frac{O(n^3 m)}{O(n^3 + n^2 m)}
=
O(n).

If \mathbf{K} admits a low-rank approximation with effective rank

r_{\text{eff}}
=
\frac{\left(\sum_{i=1}^{n} s_i\right)^2}{\sum_{i=1}^{n} s_i^2},

then the per-SNP cost reduces to O(n r_{\text{eff}}), giving

T(n,m)
=
O(n^3) + O(n m r_{\text{eff}}).

B.4 Approximation Error Propagation

Proposition B.1 (Calibration Inflation Correction). Let \chi^2_{\text{approx}} denote the test statistic obtained from an approximation-based LMM method that reuses variance parameter estimates across leave-out segments. Then there exists a calibration inflation factor \lambda_{\text{infl}} \geq 1 such that the corrected statistic

\chi^2_{\text{corrected}} = \frac{\chi^2_{\text{approx}}}{\hat{\lambda}_{\text{infl}}}

is asymptotically calibrated under the null.

Justification. The inflation arises because the variance parameter estimates are not independent of the test statistic under the null. Partitioning the genome into L leave-out segments, the inflation factor admits the approximation

\lambda_{\text{infl}} \approx 1 + \frac{c}{L},

where c depends on the trait heritability and the LD structure. As L \to \infty, \lambda_{\text{infl}} \to 1, recovering exact calibration. \square

Corollary B.2. For equity-sensitive inference, the number of leave-out segments should be chosen such that \hat{\lambda}_{\text{infl}} \leq 1 + \epsilon_{\text{cal}}, where \epsilon_{\text{cal}} is the maximum tolerable calibration error.

B.5 Near-Exact Alternative

For biobank-scale data, the near-exact LMM (NExt-LMM) exploits the low-rank structure of the genetic similarity matrix iteratively using the Hierarchical Off-Diagonal Low-Rank (HODLR) format. The Kullback–Leibler divergence between the approximated and exact estimators can be made arbitrarily small, providing rigorous error bounds that are not available for Bayesian approximations.

