Appendix D: DeLong Variance, Delta Method, and Non-Inferiority

D.1 Placement Values

Let m be the number of cases and n the number of controls. Define

V_{Ai}
=
\frac{1}{n}
\sum_{j=1}^{n}
\psi(a_i, b_j),
\qquad
V_{Bj}
=
\frac{1}{m}
\sum_{i=1}^{m}
\psi(a_i, b_j),

where

\psi(a,b)
=
\mathbf{1}\{a > b\}
+
\frac{1}{2}\mathbf{1}\{a = b\}.

The AUC estimate is

\hat{\theta}
=
\frac{1}{mn}
\sum_{i=1}^{m}\sum_{j=1}^{n}
\psi(a_i,b_j).

D.2 Variance and Covariance

DeLong's variance estimator is

\operatorname{Var}[\hat{\theta}]
=
\frac{\operatorname{Var}[V_A]}{m}
+
\frac{\operatorname{Var}[V_B]}{n}.

For two correlated AUCs,

\operatorname{Var}[\hat{\theta}^{\alpha} - \hat{\theta}^{\beta}]
=
\operatorname{Var}[\hat{\theta}^{\alpha}]
+
\operatorname{Var}[\hat{\theta}^{\beta}]
-
2\operatorname{Cov}[\hat{\theta}^{\alpha}, \hat{\theta}^{\beta}],

with

\operatorname{Cov}[\hat{\theta}^{\alpha}, \hat{\theta}^{\beta}]
=
\frac{\operatorname{Cov}[V_A^{\alpha}, V_A^{\beta}]}{m}
+
\frac{\operatorname{Cov}[V_B^{\alpha}, V_B^{\beta}]}{n}.

Under the null,

Z
=
\frac{\hat{\theta}^{\alpha} - \hat{\theta}^{\beta}}
{\sqrt{\operatorname{Var}[\hat{\theta}^{\alpha} - \hat{\theta}^{\beta}]}}
\sim \mathcal{N}(0,1).

D.3 DeLong Bias: A Careful Statement

Remark D.1 (Scoping of DeLong bias). DeLong's variance estimator is asymptotically correct under iid sampling. Its documented failure modes are not a universal positive bias but specific to:

1. Finite-sample regimes with AUC near 0.5 or 1.0.
2. Extreme case/control ratios.
3. Violation of the assumption that the models being compared are fixed and evaluated on data independent of training.

In the regime relevant to equity studies—small n_{\text{cases}} in under-served ancestries, AUC values near 0.5 or 1.0, and models trained and evaluated on the same data—the DeLong variance estimate can be substantially biased and should be treated with caution.

Corrective Remark D.1. Prior formulations claiming universal positive bias should be replaced by the scoping above.

D.4 Delta Method Alternative

Proposition D.2 (Delta Method). If the AUC is expressed as a differentiable function \theta = g(\boldsymbol{\pi}) of the confusion-matrix probabilities \boldsymbol{\pi}, and if \hat{\boldsymbol{\pi}} is asymptotically normal with covariance \Sigma_{\boldsymbol{\pi}}, then

\operatorname{Var}(\hat{\theta}) \approx \nabla g(\hat{\boldsymbol{\pi}})^{\mathsf{T}} \Sigma_{\boldsymbol{\pi}} \nabla g(\hat{\boldsymbol{\pi}}).

Justification. This is the standard delta-method result. Its empirical advantage over DeLong in equity regimes is an empirical question, not a theoretical guarantee. \square

D.5 Non-Inferiority Test

The \delta-equity criterion is

\max_{A,B} |\text{AUC}_A - \text{AUC}_B| \leq \delta.

Reformulate as

H_0:
\text{AUC}_{\text{disadvantaged}}
<
\text{AUC}_{\text{advantaged}} - \delta,

H_1:
\text{AUC}_{\text{disadvantaged}}
\geq
\text{AUC}_{\text{advantaged}} - \delta.

The test statistic is

Z_{\text{NI}}
=
\frac{
(\text{AUC}_1 - \text{AUC}_2) - \delta
}{
\operatorname{SE}(\text{AUC}_1 - \text{AUC}_2)
}.

Reject H_0 and conclude non-inferiority holds if

Z_{\text{NI}} < -z_{1-\alpha}^{\text{one-sided}}.

For a two-sided 0.05 procedure, use \alpha = 0.025 one-sided.

D.6 Equivalence Testing for k > 2

For k ancestry groups, define

\mathcal{P} = \{(i,j) : 1 \leq i < j \leq k\}.

The global equity claim is

\forall (i,j) \in \mathcal{P}: |\Delta_{ij}| \leq \delta,

with simultaneous coverage 1-\alpha. Equivalence testing via TOST:

H_0: |\text{AUC}_i - \text{AUC}_j| \geq \delta
\quad\text{vs.}\quad
H_1: |\text{AUC}_i - \text{AUC}_j| < \delta.

Reject H_0 for all (i,j) \in \mathcal{P} with family-wise error control at level \alpha.

Proposition D.3 (Family-Wise Equivalence). Under the TOST procedure with Bonferroni correction at level \alpha/|\mathcal{P}|, the family-wise error rate is controlled at level \alpha.

Proof. For each pair (i,j), the TOST procedure controls the type I error at level \alpha/|\mathcal{P}|. By the union bound,

P\left(\bigcup_{(i,j) \in \mathcal{P}} \text{Reject } H_{0,ij} \mid H_{0,ij} \text{ true}\right)
\leq
\sum_{(i,j) \in \mathcal{P}} \frac{\alpha}{|\mathcal{P}|}
=
\alpha.

Hence the family-wise error rate is controlled. \square

