Appendix E: Stratified FDR, HMP Calibration, and IHW

E.1 Within-Stratum BH

For ancestry stratum a with m_a tests, the BH procedure at level \alpha controls

\text{FDR}_a
=
\mathbb{E}
\left[
\frac{
|\mathcal{R}_a \cap \mathcal{H}_{0,a}|
}{
|\mathcal{R}_a| \vee 1
}
\right]
\leq \alpha,

provided the p-values satisfy positive regression dependence on subsets. Within a single ancestry stratum, local LD structure typically induces this condition.

E.2 Harmonic Mean P-Values

The harmonic mean p-value is

\text{HMP}
=
\frac{k}{\sum_{a=1}^{k} \frac{1}{p_a}}.

The HMP controls the strong-sense family-wise error rate (FWER), not just FDR. The claim k \cdot \text{HMP} \sim \text{Uniform}(0,1) asymptotically is not generally valid.

Proposition E.1 (HMP Invariance). The HMP is invariant to permutation of the p-values:

\text{HMP}(p_1, \ldots, p_k) = \text{HMP}(p_{\pi(1)}, \ldots, p_{\pi(k)})

for any permutation \pi of \{1,\dots,k\}.

Proof. The HMP is defined as

\text{HMP} = \frac{k}{\sum_{a=1}^{k} \frac{1}{p_a}}.

The denominator is a sum over all a, which is invariant to reordering. Hence the HMP is permutation-invariant. \square

Corollary E.2 (Permutation Calibration Invalid). Permuting the p-values does not generate a valid null distribution for the HMP, since the permuted statistic equals the observed statistic.

Proposition E.3 (LogGamma Calibration). The null distribution of the HMP is approximately LogGamma:

\text{HMP} \sim \text{LogGamma}(\alpha, \beta),

with parameters estimated from the data. The calibrated p-value is

p_{\text{HMP}} = 1 - F_{\text{LogGamma}}(\text{HMP}; \alpha, \beta).

Justification. The LogGamma approximation follows from the asymptotic distribution of the harmonic mean of independent uniform random variables under the null. The harmonicmeanp R package version 3.0 provides asymptotically exact p-values based on this distribution. \square

Corrective Remark E.1. Prior formulations invoking permutation calibration should be replaced by the LogGamma calibration above.

E.3 Independent Hypothesis Weighting

When ancestry-specific heritability, MAF distribution, or functional annotation is informative about the prior probability of association, independent hypothesis weighting can increase power relative to BH-within-strata while maintaining FDR control. This permits underpowered ancestries to be up-weighted without inflating the global false discovery rate.

Assumption E.1 (IHW Covariate Independence). The covariate used for weighting must be independent of the p-values under the null.

Proposition E.4 (IHW FDR Control). Under Assumption E.1, IHW controls the FDR at level \alpha.

Proof. The proof follows from the standard IHW argument: the weighted BH procedure with weights w(p) controls the FDR if the weights are independent of the p-values under the null and the average weight is 1. \square

