Appendix F: The Equity Feasibility Region — Formal Properties

F.1 Definition and Basic Properties

Definition F.1 (Equity Feasibility Region). Let \Theta_M be the model parameter space and \Theta_S the deployment-system parameter space. Let \Delta_r: \Theta_M \times \Theta_S \to \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0} for r = 1, \ldots, R be equity dimension functions. Let \boldsymbol{\delta} = (\delta_1, \ldots, \delta_R) \in \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^R be pre-specified tolerances. The Equity Feasibility Region is

\mathcal{V}_E(\boldsymbol{\delta}) = \left\{ (\theta_M, \theta_S) \in \Theta_M \times \Theta_S : \bigwedge_{r=1}^{R} \Delta_r(\theta_M, \theta_S) \leq \delta_r \right\}.

Proposition F.1 (Monotonicity in Tolerances). If \boldsymbol{\delta} \leq \boldsymbol{\delta}' componentwise, then \mathcal{V}_E(\boldsymbol{\delta}) \subseteq \mathcal{V}_E(\boldsymbol{\delta}').

Proof. Let \theta \in \mathcal{V}_E(\boldsymbol{\delta}). Then \Delta_r(\theta) \leq \delta_r \leq \delta_r' for all r. Hence \theta \in \mathcal{V}_E(\boldsymbol{\delta}'). \square

Proposition F.2 (Intersection Structure). Define \mathcal{V}_E^{(r)} = \{\theta : \Delta_r(\theta) \leq \delta_r\}. Then

\mathcal{V}_E = \bigcap_{r=1}^{R} \mathcal{V}_E^{(r)}.

Proof. Immediate from the definition. \square

F.2 Operational Definitions of Equity Dimensions

Dimension Metric Constraint
Discrimination \Delta_1(\theta) = \max_{A,B} \|\text{AUC}_A - \text{AUC}_B\| \leq \delta_1
Calibration \Delta_2(\theta) = \max_{A,B} \|\text{CalErr}_A - \text{CalErr}_B\| \leq \delta_2
FDR \Delta_3(\theta) = \max_{A,B} \|\text{FDR}_A - \text{FDR}_B\| \leq \delta_3
Access \Delta_4(\theta) = \max_{A,B} \|\text{Coverage}_A(\theta_S) - \text{Coverage}_B(\theta_S)\| \leq \delta_4
Utility \Delta_5(\theta) = \max_{A,B} \|\text{NetBenefit}_A(\theta_S) - \text{NetBenefit}_B(\theta_S)\| \leq \delta_5

F.3 The Benchmarking–Admissibility Distinction

Definition F.2 (Benchmarking Operator). A benchmarking operator \mathcal{B} maps a model \theta and data D to a vector of measurements:

\mathcal{B}(\theta, D) = (\Delta_1(\theta, D), \ldots, \Delta_R(\theta, D)).

Definition F.3 (Admissibility Operator). An admissibility operator \mathcal{A} maps a model \theta, data D, and tolerances \boldsymbol{\delta} to a binary decision:

\mathcal{A}(\theta, D, \boldsymbol{\delta}) =
\begin{cases}
1 & \text{if } \theta \in \mathcal{V}_E(\boldsymbol{\delta}) \\
0 & \text{otherwise.}
\end{cases}

Remark F.1 (Definitional unpacking, not a theorem). Benchmarking and admissibility are not equivalent operations.

(i) If \boldsymbol{\delta} is not pre-specified, then \mathcal{A} is undefined even though \mathcal{B} is defined. Benchmarking does not entail admissibility.

(ii) If \boldsymbol{\delta} is pre-specified but omits a benchmark B^*, a model can satisfy \mathcal{A} while failing B^*. Admissibility does not require passing all benchmarks.

Remark F.2. This is a clarifying observation, not a theorem. Its content is wholly in the definitions of \mathcal{B} and \mathcal{A}. It is stated here to make the distinction explicit; it should not be presented as a formal result.

