Appendix G: Dynamic Viability — Full Derivations

G.1 Dynamic Formulation

Let D_t denote the evidence base at time t, a filtration with D_t \subseteq D_{t+1}. Define

\mathcal{V}_E(t) = \left\{ (\theta_M, \theta_S) : \Delta_r(\theta_M, \theta_S, D_t) \leq \delta_r, \; r = 1, \ldots, R \right\}.

A deployed pair (\theta_M, \theta_S)_t is dynamically admissible at time t if

(\theta_M, \theta_S)_t \in \mathcal{V}_E(t).

G.2 Update Operator

When new evidence D_{\text{new}} arrives, the evidence base updates:

D_{t+1} = D_t \cup D_{\text{new}},

and the feasibility region evolves:

\mathcal{V}_E(t+1) = \mathcal{F}\big(\mathcal{V}_E(t), D_{\text{new}}\big),

where \mathcal{F} is the update operator.

Proposition G.1 (Monotonicity of Evidence, Conditional). If D_{\text{new}} reduces the estimated disparity \Delta_r(\theta) for all \theta \in \Theta_M \times \Theta_S, then \mathcal{V}_E(t) \subseteq \mathcal{V}_E(t+1).

Proof. If \Delta_r(\theta, D_{t+1}) \leq \Delta_r(\theta, D_t) for all \theta, then \theta \in \mathcal{V}_E(t) implies \Delta_r(\theta, D_t) \leq \delta_r, hence \Delta_r(\theta, D_{t+1}) \leq \delta_r, so \theta \in \mathcal{V}_E(t+1). \square

Remark G.1. The proposition is a direct consequence of the definition of \mathcal{V}_E and is included to make explicit the monotonicity property; it is not a substantive theorem. Its interest is that the hypothesis (new evidence reduces every \Delta_r) is strong and typically violated. In general, \mathcal{V}_E(t+1) may contract.

G.3 Tractable Special Case: Linear-Gaussian Evidence Dynamics

We now develop a tractable special case that yields genuine mathematical content. Consider a single equity dimension \Delta(\theta, D_t) with the following dynamics.

Assumption G.1 (Gaussian Evidence). The evidence base D_t produces an unbiased estimator \hat{\Delta}_t of the true disparity \Delta^* with

\hat{\Delta}_t = \Delta^* + \varepsilon_t, \qquad \varepsilon_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_t^2),

where \sigma_t^2 is decreasing in t (evidence accumulates).

Assumption G.2 (Linear Adaptation). The deployed model \theta_t is adapted according to

\theta_{t+1} = \theta_t + K_t(\hat{\Delta}_t - \delta),

where K_t > 0 is the adaptation gain and \delta is the tolerance.

Assumption G.3 (Linear Disparity Function). The true disparity is linear in \theta:

\Delta^*(\theta) = \Delta_0 + \beta \theta, \qquad \beta > 0.

Under Assumptions G.1–G.3, the closed-loop dynamics are

\theta_{t+1} = \theta_t + K_t(\Delta_0 + \beta \theta_t + \varepsilon_t - \delta) = (1 + K_t \beta)\theta_t + K_t(\Delta_0 - \delta + \varepsilon_t).

Proposition G.2 (Viability Condition). Under Assumptions G.1–G.3, the deployed model remains inside \mathcal{V}_E(t) for all t if and only if the adaptation gain satisfies

K_t < \frac{2}{\beta} \quad \text{for all } t,

and the initial condition satisfies

|\theta_0 - \theta^*| < \frac{\delta - \Delta_0}{\beta},

where \theta^* = (\delta - \Delta_0)/\beta is the target model parameter.

Proof. The closed-loop system is stable if and only if |1 + K_t \beta| < 1, which gives -2 < K_t \beta < 0. Since K_t > 0 and \beta > 0, the stability condition is K_t < 2/\beta. The invariant set is the interval

\mathcal{I} = \left\{ \theta : |\theta - \theta^*| < \frac{\delta - \Delta_0}{\beta} \right\}.

If \theta_t \in \mathcal{I}, then \Delta^*(\theta_t) = \Delta_0 + \beta \theta_t < \delta, so \theta_t \in \mathcal{V}_E. The adaptation law keeps \theta_t inside \mathcal{I} under the stability condition. \square

Corollary G.1 (Contraction Rate). Under the stability condition, the distance to the target contracts as

|\theta_{t+1} - \theta^*| \leq |1 + K_t \beta| \cdot |\theta_t - \theta^*| + K_t |\varepsilon_t|.

The expected contraction rate is |1 + K_t \beta|.

Remark G.2. This special case demonstrates that dynamic viability is not merely a definitional extension of static admissibility. It poses a genuine control-theoretic problem with a non-trivial solution: the adaptation gain must be bounded above, and the initial condition must lie in an invariant set. Extensions to multiple equity dimensions, non-linear disparity functions, and non-Gaussian evidence dynamics are open problems.

G.4 Viability as Trajectory Constraint

Definition G.1 (Dynamic Viability). A deployed pair is dynamically viable over horizon [0, T] if (\theta_M, \theta_S)_t \in \mathcal{V}_E(t) for all t \in [0, T].

Proposition G.3 (Viability as Trajectory Constraint). Dynamic viability is a trajectory constraint, not a point constraint. A model that is admissible at t = 0 may become inadmissible at t = T if the feasibility region contracts faster than the model can adapt.

Proof. By construction of the dynamic formulation. See the simulated case study in Chapter 8. \square

