Appendix H: Governance Mechanism Design — Formal Analysis

H.1 Three-Party δ-Setting

Definition H.1 (Three-Party δ-Setting).

1. Regulatory floor. A regulator sets a minimum floor \boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{reg}} \in \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^R.
2. Clinical-society specification. A clinical society specifies \boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{clin}} \geq \boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{reg}}.
3. Affected-community ratification. A standing panel ratifies \boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{comm}} \geq \boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{clin}}.

The admissible set is

\mathcal{V}_E(\boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{comm}}) = \left\{ \theta : \bigwedge_{r=1}^R \Delta_r(\theta) \leq \delta_{\text{comm},r} \right\}.

H.2 Mechanism-Design Analysis

We model the three-party process as a sequential game with players: sponsor S, clinical society C, and community panel P.

Assumption H.1 (Sponsor Objective). The sponsor chooses \theta_M to minimize cost subject to \theta \in \mathcal{V}_E(\boldsymbol{\delta}):

\min_{\theta_M} \; C(\theta_M) \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \theta \in \mathcal{V}_E(\boldsymbol{\delta}).

Assumption H.2 (Clinical Society Objective). The clinical society chooses \boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{clin}} to maximize clinical validity subject to \boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{clin}} \geq \boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{reg}}:

\max_{\boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{clin}}} \; V(\boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{clin}}) \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{clin}} \geq \boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{reg}}.

Assumption H.3 (Community Panel Objective). The community panel chooses \boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{comm}} to minimize access and utility disparities subject to \boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{comm}} \geq \boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{clin}}:

\min_{\boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{comm}}} \; \left(\Delta_4(\boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{comm}}) + \Delta_5(\boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{comm}})\right) \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{comm}} \geq \boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{clin}}.

Proposition H.1 (Exogeneity Condition). The three-party process produces \boldsymbol{\delta} credibly exogenous to the sponsor if and only if:

1. The sponsor cannot influence the selection of clinical-society members.
2. The sponsor cannot influence the selection of community-panel members.
3. The regulatory floor is binding: \delta_{\text{comm},r} \geq \delta_{\text{reg},r} for all r.
4. The community panel has authority to tighten \boldsymbol{\delta} without sponsor veto.

Justification. If conditions 1–2 fail, the sponsor can capture one or more parties and shift \boldsymbol{\delta} toward leniency. If condition 3 fails, the regulatory floor is non-binding and the sponsor can shop for a lenient clinical specification. If condition 4 fails, the community panel cannot correct for clinical-society capture. The conditions are jointly necessary and sufficient for exogeneity in the sense that no sponsor strategy can shift \boldsymbol{\delta} below the regulatory floor. \square

Remark H.1. The proposition is a preliminary characterization. A full mechanism-design analysis would specify the sponsor's optimization problem, the clinical society's objective function, the community panel's objective function, and derive equilibrium \boldsymbol{\delta} under various informational assumptions. We flag this as an open problem in §9.2.

H.3 Capture Scenarios

Scenario H.1 (Clinical-Society Capture). If the sponsor funds the clinical society, the clinical society may set \boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{clin}} close to \boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{reg}}, leaving the community panel with little room to tighten.

Scenario H.2 (Community-Panel Capture). If the sponsor influences the selection of community-panel members, the panel may ratify \boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{comm}} = \boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{clin}}, defeating the purpose of ratification.

Scenario H.3 (Regulatory Capture). If the sponsor lobbies the regulator, the regulatory floor \boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{reg}} may be set leniently, rendering the entire structure ineffective.

Remark H.2. The three-party structure is designed to make capture of any single party insufficient to shift \boldsymbol{\delta} below the regulatory floor. However, simultaneous capture of multiple parties remains a vulnerability. Formalizing the conditions under which simultaneous capture is possible is an open problem.

