=
\mu
+
\sum_{a=1}^{k}
\beta_{ja}
\mathbf{1}\{a_{ij} = a\}
g_{ij}
+
\mathbf{z}_i^{\mathsf{T}}\boldsymbol{\gamma}
+
\epsilon_i.

Tractor-Mix extends this to related individuals by incorporating a random effect with covariance \mathbf{K}.

J.2 Cross-Ancestry Genetic Correlation

The bivariate LMM is

\begin{pmatrix}
\mathbf{y}_1 \\
\mathbf{y}_2
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
\mathbf{X}_1 & \mathbf{0} \\
\mathbf{0} & \mathbf{X}_2
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
\boldsymbol{\beta}_1 \\
\boldsymbol{\beta}_2
\end{pmatrix}
+
\begin{pmatrix}
\mathbf{g}_1 \\
\mathbf{g}_2
\end{pmatrix}
+
\begin{pmatrix}
\boldsymbol{\epsilon}_1 \\
\boldsymbol{\epsilon}_2
\end{pmatrix},

with

\begin{pmatrix}
\mathbf{g}_1 \\
\mathbf{g}_2
\end{pmatrix}
\sim
\mathcal{N}
\left(
\mathbf{0},
\begin{pmatrix}
\sigma_{g_1}^2 \mathbf{K}_1 &
\rho_g \sigma_{g_1}\sigma_{g_2} \mathbf{K}_{12} \\
\rho_g \sigma_{g_1}\sigma_{g_2} \mathbf{K}_{12}^{\mathsf{T}} &
\sigma_{g_2}^2 \mathbf{K}_2
\end{pmatrix}
\right).

J.3 Local Ancestry Estimation Error

Let I = \mathbf{1}\{a_{ij}=a\} be the true indicator and \hat{I} = \mathbf{1}\{\hat{a}_{ij}=a\} the estimated indicator. Suppose

\text{sensitivity} = se = P(\hat{I}=1 \mid I=1),
\qquad
\text{specificity} = sp = P(\hat{I}=0 \mid I=0).

Let p = P(I=1). Then

E[\hat{I}] = p\, se + (1-p)(1-sp),

\operatorname{Var}(\hat{I}) = E[\hat{I}](1-E[\hat{I}]),

\operatorname{Cov}(\hat{I}, I) = p(1-p)(se + sp - 1).

If the true model is y = \beta I + \epsilon and the fitted model uses \hat{I}, the probability limit of the estimated coefficient is

\hat{\beta}
\to
\beta
\frac{\operatorname{Cov}(\hat{I}, I)}{\operatorname{Var}(\hat{I})}
=
\beta
\frac{p(1-p)(se+sp-1)}
{E[\hat{I}](1-E[\hat{I}])}.

Thus switch errors and phasing errors attenuate or reverse ancestry-specific effect estimates.

Corrective Remark J.1. Prior formulations assert a "10–30%" bias range. This quantitative claim should be replaced by the analytic expression above, with simulation-derived magnitudes for specific (se, sp, p) regimes.

J.4 Tract-Structure Failure

Proposition J.1 (Tract-Structure Distortion). Per-site accuracy of local ancestry inference does not guarantee accurate tract structure. The number of estimated ancestry tracts may be inflated by a factor

\eta = \frac{\mathbb{E}[\hat{N}_{\text{tracts}}]}{\mathbb{E}[N_{\text{tracts}}]},

and the estimated admixture time may be correspondingly biased:

\hat{t}_{\text{admix}} = \eta \cdot t_{\text{admix}}.

For the most accurate methods, \eta can be as large as 78.8, implying an admixture time 61.2\times too old.

Justification. This is an empirical finding from a systematic study across F_{ST} from 0.0022 to 0.243. The mechanism is that per-site accuracy conceals a failure of tract structure: the most accurate method per site produces too many tracts, implying an admixture time that is too old. \square

