Appendix M: Proofs of Main Results

M.1 Proof of Proposition F.1 (Monotonicity in Tolerances)

Proposition F.1. If \boldsymbol{\delta} \leq \boldsymbol{\delta}' componentwise, then \mathcal{V}_E(\boldsymbol{\delta}) \subseteq \mathcal{V}_E(\boldsymbol{\delta}').

Proof. Let \theta \in \mathcal{V}_E(\boldsymbol{\delta}). Then \Delta_r(\theta) \leq \delta_r \leq \delta_r' for all r. Hence \theta \in \mathcal{V}_E(\boldsymbol{\delta}'). \square

M.2 Proof of Proposition F.2 (Intersection Structure)

Proposition F.2. \mathcal{V}_E = \bigcap_{r=1}^{R} \mathcal{V}_E^{(r)}.

Proof. Immediate from the definition. \square

M.3 Proof of Proposition G.2 (Viability Condition)

Proposition G.2. Under Assumptions G.1–G.3, the deployed model remains inside \mathcal{V}_E(t) for all t if and only if K_t < 2/\beta for all t and |\theta_0 - \theta^*| < (\delta - \Delta_0)/\beta.

Proof. The closed-loop system is stable if and only if |1 + K_t \beta| < 1, which gives -2 < K_t \beta < 0. Since K_t > 0 and \beta > 0, the stability condition is K_t < 2/\beta. The invariant set is the interval

\mathcal{I} = \left\{ \theta : |\theta - \theta^*| < \frac{\delta - \Delta_0}{\beta} \right\}.

If \theta_t \in \mathcal{I}, then \Delta^*(\theta_t) = \Delta_0 + \beta \theta_t < \delta, so \theta_t \in \mathcal{V}_E. The adaptation law keeps \theta_t inside \mathcal{I} under the stability condition. \square

M.4 Proof of Proposition H.1 (Exogeneity Condition)

Proposition H.1. The three-party process produces \boldsymbol{\delta} credibly exogenous to the sponsor if and only if conditions 1–4 hold.

Justification. If conditions 1–2 fail, the sponsor can capture one or more parties and shift \boldsymbol{\delta} toward leniency. If condition 3 fails, the regulatory floor is non-binding and the sponsor can shop for a lenient clinical specification. If condition 4 fails, the community panel cannot correct for clinical-society capture. The conditions are jointly necessary and sufficient for exogeneity in the sense that no sponsor strategy can shift \boldsymbol{\delta} below the regulatory floor. \square

M.5 Proof of Proposition D.3 (Family-Wise Equivalence)

Proposition D.3. Under the TOST procedure with Bonferroni correction at level \alpha/|\mathcal{P}|, the family-wise error rate is controlled at level \alpha.

Proof. For each pair (i,j) \in \mathcal{P}, the TOST procedure controls the type I error at level \alpha/|\mathcal{P}|. By the union bound,

P\left(\bigcup_{(i,j) \in \mathcal{P}} \{\text{Reject } H_{0,ij}\} \mid H_{0,ij} \text{ true}\right)
\leq
\sum_{(i,j) \in \mathcal{P}} \frac{\alpha}{|\mathcal{P}|}
=
\alpha.

Hence the family-wise error rate is controlled at level \alpha. \square

M.6 Proof of Proposition E.1 (HMP Invariance)

Proposition E.1. The HMP is invariant to permutation of the p-values.

Proof. For any permutation \pi of \{1,\dots,k\},

\text{HMP}(p_{\pi(1)}, \ldots, p_{\pi(k)})
=
\frac{k}{\sum_{a=1}^{k} \frac{1}{p_{\pi(a)}}}
=
\frac{k}{\sum_{a=1}^{k} \frac{1}{p_a}}
=
\text{HMP}(p_1, \ldots, p_k),

where the second equality follows from the fact that summation is commutative. \square

M.7 Proof of Proposition C.1 (Population-Conditional Bayes QC Threshold)

Proposition C.1. Under differentiability and an interior optimum, the population-specific threshold \tau_i^* satisfies

\frac{f_i^1(\tau_i^*)}{f_i^0(\tau_i^*)} = \frac{c_\beta}{c_\alpha}.

Proof. The first-order condition for the population-specific loss L_i(\tau) = c_\alpha F_i^1(\tau) + c_\beta (1 - F_i^0(\tau)) is

\frac{\partial L_i}{\partial \tau}
=
c_\alpha f_i^1(\tau) - c_\beta f_i^0(\tau) = 0.

Rearranging,

c_\alpha f_i^1(\tau_i^*) = c_\beta f_i^0(\tau_i^*).

Dividing both sides by c_\alpha f_i^0(\tau_i^*),

\frac{f_i^1(\tau_i^*)}{f_i^0(\tau_i^*)} = \frac{c_\beta}{c_\alpha}.

The second-order condition requires that the derivative of the likelihood ratio is negative at \tau_i^*, ensuring a minimum. \square

