Definition P.2 (Access Disparity). For ancestry A, define

\text{Coverage}_A(\theta_S) = \frac{\text{uptake}_A(\theta_S)}{\text{eligible}_A},

where \text{uptake}_A(\theta_S) is the number of individuals in ancestry A who receive the genomic test, and \text{eligible}_A is the number eligible.

Definition P.3 (Access Disparity Metric).

\Delta_4(\theta_S) = \max_{A,B} |\text{Coverage}_A(\theta_S) - \text{Coverage}_B(\theta_S)|.

P.3 Utility Disparity as a Function of θ_S

Definition P.4 (Net Benefit). For ancestry A,

\text{NetBenefit}_A(\theta_S) = Q_A(\theta_S) - \frac{C_A(\theta_S)}{\lambda},

where Q_A(\theta_S) is the QALYs gained, C_A(\theta_S) is the cost, and \lambda is the willingness-to-pay threshold.

Definition P.5 (Utility Disparity Metric).

\Delta_5(\theta_S) = \max_{A,B} |\text{NetBenefit}_A(\theta_S) - \text{NetBenefit}_B(\theta_S)|.

P.4 Worked Example: Same PRS, Two Health Systems

Health System 1 (Universal Coverage).

\theta_S^{(1)} = (\text{universal reimbursement}, \text{provider training}, \text{community outreach}, \text{public insurance}).

\Delta_4(\theta_S^{(1)}) = 0.02, \quad \Delta_5(\theta_S^{(1)}) = 0.05.

Health System 2 (Means-Tested Coverage).

\theta_S^{(2)} = (\text{means-tested reimbursement}, \text{limited provider training}, \text{no community outreach}, \text{mixed insurance}).

\Delta_4(\theta_S^{(2)}) = 0.15, \quad \Delta_5(\theta_S^{(2)}) = 0.20.

Verdict. If \delta_4 = 0.10 and \delta_5 = 0.15:

· (\theta_M, \theta_S^{(1)}) \in \mathcal{V}_E (admissible)
· (\theta_M, \theta_S^{(2)}) \notin \mathcal{V}_E (inadmissible)

Remark P.1. This worked example demonstrates that admissibility is a property of the pair (\theta_M, \theta_S), not of the model alone. The same PRS may be admissible in one health system and inadmissible in another.

