Appendix Q: Numerical Convergence of the Bayes Threshold Solver

Q.1 Newton–Raphson Iteration

The Bayes threshold \tau_i^* satisfies

\ell_i(\tau) = \log f_i^1(\tau) - \log f_i^0(\tau) = \log(c_\beta/c_\alpha).

Proposition Q.1 (Newton–Raphson Convergence). Let \ell_i(\tau) be twice continuously differentiable. If \ell_i'(\tau_i^*) = \log(c_\beta/c_\alpha) and \ell_i''(\tau_i^*) < 0, then the Newton–Raphson iteration

\tau_i^{(t+1)} = \tau_i^{(t)} - \frac{\ell_i'(\tau_i^{(t)}) - \log(c_\beta/c_\alpha)}{\ell_i''(\tau_i^{(t)})}

converges quadratically to \tau_i^* in a neighborhood of the solution.

Proof. This is the standard Newton–Raphson convergence theorem. The conditions \ell_i'(\tau_i^*) = \log(c_\beta/c_\alpha) and \ell_i''(\tau_i^*) < 0 ensure that \tau_i^* is a simple root and that the iteration is locally contractive. \square

Corollary Q.1 (Initialization). A consistent initialization is \tau_i^{(0)} = F_i^1{}^{-1}(c_\beta/(c_\alpha + c_\beta)), which is the threshold that equalizes the weighted error rates under the null.

Q.2 Convergence Diagnostic

Monitor the relative change:

\text{rel.change} = \frac{|\tau_i^{(t+1)} - \tau_i^{(t)}|}{|\tau_i^{(t)}| + \epsilon}.

Terminate when \text{rel.change} < 10^{-6}.

