Appendix S: Open Problems — Formal Statements

Problem S.1 (Governance Incentive Analysis). Formalize the three-party \boldsymbol{\delta}-setting process as a mechanism-design problem. Under what conditions does the process produce \boldsymbol{\delta} that is credibly exogenous to the sponsor? What are the equilibrium strategies, and do they produce admissible models?

Problem S.2 (Trajectory Viability). Characterize the set of (\theta_M, \theta_S)_0 \in \mathcal{V}_E(0) from which a dynamically viable trajectory exists under a specified evidence-generating process. Proposition G.2 solves the linear-Gaussian case; the general problem for multiple equity dimensions and non-linear dynamics remains open.

Problem S.3 (Transportability of \Delta_r across Deployment Systems). Given that \Delta_4 and \Delta_5 depend on \theta_S, how do we transport estimates of \Delta_4, \Delta_5 from one health system to another? What is the identification strategy?

Problem S.4 (Non-Nested Evidence). The filtration assumption D_t \subseteq D_{t+1} fails when evidence is retracted or contradicted. How should \mathcal{V}_E(t) update under non-nested evidence?

Problem S.5 (Simultaneous Capture). The three-party structure is designed to make capture of any single party insufficient to shift \boldsymbol{\delta} below the regulatory floor. Under what conditions is simultaneous capture of multiple parties possible, and how can the structure be robustified?

Problem S.6 (Multi-Dimensional Viability). Proposition G.2 solves the viability problem for a single equity dimension. Extend to R > 1 dimensions with coupled dynamics.

Problem S.7 (Non-Linear Disparity Functions). Assumption G.3 assumes linearity of \Delta^*(\theta). Extend the viability analysis to non-linear disparity functions.

Problem S.8 (Non-Gaussian Evidence). Assumption G.1 assumes Gaussian evidence. Extend to heavy-tailed or skewed evidence distributions.

